title: treeref panel
tags: Panel [[TreeRef forest]]
kind: reference
summary: Walks a tree of metarecords — the files, or a tag hierarchy — from its roots down; adds nodes to it, and lists what points at a node.

Open it with `panel:open here treeref` (<<key "panel:open here treeref" "t t">>). It
walks one [[TreeRef forest]] — the tree a `tree_ref` field draws — the way the file
manager walks the disk, for any such field: `mfr_path`, the files, or a hierarchy of
tags ([[Hierarchical tags]]).

The ''field'' button chooses the tree, among the repository's `tree_ref` fields;
<<key "treeref:set field" Shift+f>> (`treeref:set field`) asks for it in the command
input. It starts on `mfr_path` (`[panel-defaults.treeref]`, [[GUI configuration]]),
and changing it goes back to the roots.

!! Walking

The panel shows the roots, then the children of the node it was taken into; the line
above them gives the path to it, each piece a link back. Whether a node has children
is not known beforehand: going into a leaf shows an empty list.

|!Key |!Does |
|<<key "treeref:next" "↓ or j">> / <<key "treeref:prev" "↑ or k">>, <<key "treeref:first" Home>> / <<key "treeref:last" End>> |move the cursor (`End`: the last child loaded) |
|<<key "treeref:descend" "Enter or →">>, or a double click |go into the node |
|<<key "treeref:parent" "Backspace or ←">> |back to its parent |
|<<key "treeref:root" "/">> |back to the roots |
|<<key "treeref:find" f>> |jump to a child by name, among those loaded ([[Jumping to an entry]]) |
|<<key "treeref:refresh" r>> |read the fields and the children again |

The node under the cursor is the workspace's selection, so [[metarecord-detail panel]]
beside it shows it; its field, uuid and path are also published as `selected_treeref`
([[Workspace variables]]). Children are loaded 200 at a time (`[page-size]`), more as
the list scrolls; the roots come all at once.

<<key "metarecord:remove" Delete>>, <<key "metarecord:trash" Ctrl+Delete>> and
<<key "metarecord:delete" Shift+Delete>> act on the selected node's metarecord
([[Sending files to the trash]]). In the `mfr_path` tree a node is a file or a folder,
and its right-click menu has the file's actions too ([[Context menus]]).

!! Adding a node

<<key "treeref:add" Ctrl+n>> (`treeref:add`, the ''+ add'' button) creates a
metarecord placed under the node the panel is ''in'' — next to the rows shown, not
under the cursor; at the roots, it is a new root. It asks two things:

# the ''schema type'' to create it with, completed over the types the schema declares ([[Schema]]): its template fields are filled in. Left blank, the metarecord holds its position and nothing else; a type the schema does not have is refused.
# its ''name'', one piece of path — a `/` in it is refused ([[Tree names]]).

The new node gets the cursor.

!! What points at this node?

A tree raises the question at once — what is tagged with this? The second row of the
toolbar answers it by writing a query:

* ''ref'' — the `ref` field to follow back into the tree: `tag` unless configured otherwise (<<key "treeref:set ref-field" e>>, `treeref:set ref-field`);
* ''scope'' — `exact`, the node itself, or `+ descendants`, the node and everything below it (<<key "treeref:toggle scope" s>>);
* and the query the two make for the node under the cursor:

```
tag -> (path = "music/jazz")      the node itself
tag -> (path =>* "music/jazz")    the node and every tag below it
```

<<key "treeref:list-refs" l>> (`treeref:list-refs`, or ''List what tag points at here''
in the right-click menu) runs it in a [[metarecord-list panel]] opened in the
''other'' slot: the tree stays in view for the next node. <<key "treeref:list-refs new" "b l">>
runs it in a new workspace instead, <<key "treeref:list-refs here" "i l">> in place of the
tree ([[open targets|Panel slots and layout]]). The query lands in the
list's normal DSL, shown and frozen, where it can be narrowed or combined with
anything else ([[Following references]]).

!! As a value picker

Opened to pick a `tree_ref` parent ([[Value picker]]), the panel starts on the field
being edited, and a node's right-click menu starts with ''Pick this folder'' or
''Pick this node''.
